     1	//! Function registry — name → implementation dispatch.
     2	//!
     3	//! Names are stored uppercase per Excel canon. Lookup is case-insensitive: the caller
     4	//! can pass `"sum"`, `"SUM"`, or `"Sum"` and get the same function.
     5	//!
     6	//! ## Storage shape (W5-96 / Phase 4.7.B unification)
     7	//!
     8	//! Pre-W5-96 the registry held three parallel `HashMap`s, one per function
     9	//! tier (`ScalarFn`, `RangeAwareFn`, `ContextAwareFn`). Phase 4.7 needs a
    10	//! fourth tier (array-returning functions for SEQUENCE / FILTER /
    11	//! TRANSPOSE), and Codex design review HIGH-1 flagged the proliferation:
    12	//! the right move at this point is to **unify** the dispatch table into
    13	//! one map keyed by name, valued by a `RegisteredFn` enum that tags the
    14	//! tier. Eval-site dispatch becomes one HashMap lookup + one `match`.
    15	//!
    16	//! This commit (W5-96) restructures STORAGE without changing the public
    17	//! `register_*` / `lookup_*` API surface: legacy callers still call
    18	//! `register("SUM", sum_fn)` and `lookup("SUM")` exactly as before. The
    19	//! lookup methods filter on the enum variant (e.g. `lookup_range_aware`
    20	//! returns `Some(raf)` only if the registered fn is
    21	//! `RegisteredFn::RangeAware(raf)`). Eval-site migration to a single
    22	//! match-on-enum lands in W5-101 (Phase 4.7.G) when the array-eval path
    23	//! actually needs it.
    24	//!
    25	//! New array-returning functions register via `register_unified(name,
    26	//! FunctionFn)` and are stored as `RegisteredFn::Unified(_)`. Eval-site
    27	//! dispatch in W5-101 will route them through a new `match` arm that
    28	//! returns `EvalResult::Array(_)` to the runtime.
